                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "7/10\n\nA fun mash-up of metroidvania-style exploration, run'n'gun gameplay and bullet hell elements.\n\nDespite the description above, Greedy Guns is as simple as it gets: you run, shoot enemies on the way and collect money to buy weapons. Besides that, there are a few secrets to be found and some abilities that you'll get that will then allow you to explore sections of the game that were earlier blocked off.\nAnd as far as simplicity goes, it works very well in this game - it's fun the way it is, while also presenting at least some challenge due to the bullet hell elements. And, of course, it's double as fun when played coop.\n\nThe biggest problem with the game is that it's awfully short for a metroidvania game. There isn't much exploration to be done, even after you've gotten new abilities. Mostly, re-exploring earlier sections will grant health pieces (4 pieces increases the character's max HP) or money and there is an extra weapon to be found as well, but that's about it.\n\nIt also doesn't help that, for a game called \"Greedy Guns\", collecting money is actually quite irrelevant. It's easy enough to collect money to buy new weapons and there is nothing else to be done with money then.\n\nGreedy Guns sadly suffers from wasted potential - if the game was longer, exploration was more meaningful and there were more uses for collected money, the game would have been MUCH better.\n\nHowever, issues aside, one important thing to mention is that the game has local coop (as mentioned above). So while the single player experience is short and somewhat lackluster, the game is potentially a lot more fun if played with a friend since, as mentioned, the game's premise is simple enough, which makes it perfect for having two characters going around shooting the hell out of enemies while dodging bullets.\n\nI'm hoping the game will have updates in the future that will include more content and then make good use of the game's potential.",
